From: Funkyhacks <funkyhacks@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 21 Aug 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 4/6] core/crypto: SecretBytes never prints key bytes in repr/str

SecretBytes subclasses bytearray and inherited its __repr__/__str__, which
print every byte of the buffer. If a SecretBytes is ever included in a log
line, f-string, or traceback, the live key material is written to the log
even though key_scope() zeroizes the buffer later. Mask repr/str to a fixed
redacted form; bytes()/len()/iteration still work for the crypto layer.

Refs: docs/SECURITY.md finding (SecretBytes repr leak), THREAT-MODEL.md T17.

---
--- a/app/core/crypto.py
+++ b/app/core/crypto.py
@@ -51,6 +51,15 @@
     "key zeroization" standing default.
     """
 
+    def __repr__(self) -> str:
+        # Never dump key material: the inherited bytearray.__repr__ prints
+        # every byte, which leaks live key material into logs / f-strings /
+        # tracebacks if the object is ever formatted (SECURITY.md finding:
+        # SecretBytes repr leak). Mask instead; len() is safe to expose.
+        return f"<SecretBytes {len(self)} bytes [REDACTED]>"
+
+    __str__ = __repr__
+
     def zeroize(self) -> None:
         """Overwrite every byte in place. Best-effort under CPython, but the
         buffer is no longer reusable and any retained reference is wiped."""
